Natural key versus surrogate key.

When you pick a primary key, you face a choice between two philosophies. Use an identifier the business already has — or invent one. That's natural key versus surrogate key.

A natural key is a column drawn from real business data that already uniquely identifies a record — an email, a P-A-N, an I-S-B-N, a product S-K-U. It has business meaning, and it exists out in the real world. Its weakness is twofold: real-world identifiers can change, and they're often text — bigger and slower to index and join.

A surrogate key is the opposite. It's an artificial identifier the system generates — typically an auto-incrementing integer, like customer i-d one-thousand-and-one. It has no business meaning, it doesn't exist outside the database, and, crucially, it never changes. It isn't derived from the row's data at all.

Here's the problem a surrogate solves. An online store keys its customer dimension on the natural key — the email. Anita signs up as anita-at-old-dot-com and places some orders. Later she changes her email to anita-at-new-dot-com. With the natural key alone, the key just changed — so her two orders now look like two completely different people. But give her a surrogate key, and her identity stays stable while the email becomes just an attribute. Both orders still map to customer one-thousand-and-one — one customer.

And that same stable surrogate is what makes slowly-changing-dimension type two possible. You can keep several historical rows for the same real customer, each with its own surrogate, all tracing back to one business identity.

So, side by side: the natural key comes from business data, has meaning, but can change and is often slow text. The surrogate is system-generated, meaningless, permanent, a small fast integer, and it integrates cleanly across sources. The best practice: in warehouse dimensions, use a surrogate key as the primary key, and keep the natural key as an attribute, so you can still trace a row back to its source system. Why the warehouse leans so hard on surrogates — beyond this one example — is our closing section.
